 "caveats": [
  "IC observations are one per rebalance week; the trailing mean is a mean of rank ICs, not a regression slope, so it is comparable across factors but not to a factor-return premium.",
  "the lagged arm pays the same 8bp/side but its decisions are noisier by construction (a sign can flip on a single year of data) -- turnover is printed so the reader can see that cost before trusting a gap.",
  "the 2026 year is partial (through the panel end), so a factor that flipped this year is judged on a fraction of a year.",
  "the two diagnostic arms are chosen AFTER the yearly table was read, so their gaps carry selection risk: six factors were examined and only the flattering flip is printed. drawdown_60 is the awkward case -- its pooled mean IC is positive (+0.0398) while only 3 of 7 years agree, so flipping it follows the year count against the pooled evidence. Neither selector is trustworthy; that disagreement is the finding, not a recommendation.",
  "diagnostic arms ride one decision phase (the same 20-bar grid as production). Phase spread on this book has been measured at ~0.5pp, so an 87bp gap is not yet separable from the grid -- a phase sweep is the named next step, no production change is claimed here."
 ],
